# Document types and methods (learning DB)

Every new or edited Java **type** and **non-trivial method** must have a short description a student can read without opening another file. Pair this with `.cursor/rules/explain-code-comments.mdc` (why on non-obvious lines). Do **not** replace why-comments with empty “what” restatements.

## What must be documented

| Element | Required |
|---------|----------|
| `public` / package-visible **class, interface, enum, record** | Class-level Javadoc: role, layering (what this is **not**), invariants |
| `public` / package-visible **methods** (including constructors used as API) | Javadoc: purpose, important params/returns, thrown cases that matter |
| Package-private helpers that encode real policy | Same as public — this is a teaching codebase |
| Simple getters/setters that only return a field | One-line Javadoc OK, or inherit; no paragraph noise |
| Test methods | Prefer a clear method name; Javadoc only when the scenario is non-obvious |

When you **edit** a method’s behavior, update its Javadoc in the same change.

## Description style

- Lead with **role** (“RAM cache of disk pages”), not restating the name.
- Say what layer owns this vs nearby layers (BufferPool ≠ LockManager ≠ WAL ≠ HeapPage).
- Call out crash/data-loss/concurrency consequences when relevant.
- Keep it tight: a few sentences, not a novel.

## Examples (when necessary)

Add a short usage example when the call order, lifecycle, or layering is easy to get wrong:

- Pin → latch → mutate → markDirty → unlatch → unpin
- Create file before `newPage`
- Codec encode/decode round-trip
- Anything with “never hold until COMMIT” or similar protocol

**Prefer** a `{@code ...}` or `<pre>` block in the **type** Javadoc (or on the main entry method).

**Alternatively**, if the example spans several types or would clutter the type header, put a single block comment at the **end of the file** (before the closing brace of the top-level type, or after it for package-info-style notes in the same file):

```java
/*
 * Example — pin, edit, flush:
 *
 *   BufferFrame f = pool.pin(new PageId("t.ibd", 0));
 *   pool.latchExclusive(f);
 *   f.data()[0] = 1;
 *   pool.markDirty(f);
 *   pool.unlatch(f);
 *   pool.unpin(f);
 *   pool.flush(new PageId("t.ibd", 0));
 */
```

Do **not** scatter huge tutorial examples between every method. One solid example per type (or end-of-file) is enough unless a method has its own tricky protocol.

## Good / bad

```java
// BAD — restates the signature
/** Pins a page. */
BufferFrame pin(PageId pageId);

// GOOD — role + caller duty + what this is not
/**
 * Load or hit {@code pageId} and increment pin count so the frame cannot be evicted.
 * Caller must {@link #unpin} when done. This is not a SQL lock — use {@code LockManager}
 * for table/row concurrency; latch separately while touching {@link BufferFrame#data()}.
 */
BufferFrame pin(PageId pageId);
```

```java
// BAD — example between every method (noise)
/** ... */ void unpin(...) { }
/* Example unpin: pool.unpin(f); */
/** ... */ void flush(...) { }

// GOOD — one example on the type or at end of file
```

## Scope

Applies when writing or substantially editing Java under this repo (server, tests). Do not invent docs for generated code. Skip one-line private lambdas and obvious test fixtures.
